  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... An active weather pattern is likely from late \non Thanksgiving into this weekend. Forecast confidence on any \nparticular weather system is low, but travelers should continue \nto monitor this period for potential travel disruptions. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe general weather pattern from Thanksgiving through the weekend is \nfavorable for active weather. This will likely impact holiday \ntravel. \n\nAn upper level system is moving through the Rocky Mountains currently\nand will continue to move east and deepen on the lee side of \nthe Rockies on Tuesday afternoon. This will result in \ncyclogenesis off of the coast of Florida Wednesday and then the \nsystem moves up the eastern seaboard on Thanksgiving day. There \nis a general consistency among global models that from there the\nprimary low will travel inland and begin to occlude with the \nupper low while a secondary low still spin off from the parent \nlow and travel northeast, closer to the coast.\n\nModels have been consistently holding precipitation back until\nlate Thursday evening at the earliest, with a majority of \nguidance indicating onset of precipitation will be early Friday\nmorning. 850mb temps looks to be well above 0C at the onset of \nprecipitation but the surface temps will be hanging right around\nthe freezing mark, especially in northern and central areas. \nBased on the latest soundings and NBM/s probability of ptypes it\nappears that there could be some cold air damming setting up \nover favorable areas in northern and central Maine, leading to a\nperiod of wintry mix before switching over to all rain shortly \nafter sunrise. There is still very high uncertainty with this \nstorm at this time. If the precipitation arrives later than \ncurrently forecast, chances for wintry mix will be near zero. If\nthe system is faster than anticipated then there will be a \nlonger window for wintry mix. Additional chances for wintry mix/\nsnow showers are possible Sunday night and Monday, though \nconfidence is low at this time. \n\nIn summary, it is still early, but be aware of the potential for \nactive weather that could impact travel Thanksgiving evening through \nSunday. Continue to check back for updates.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Noticeably colder Tuesday with gusty northwest\nwinds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front will cross the region this afternoon through the \nevening, ushering cold air across the region. Gusty northwest \nwinds will develop overnight then persist Tuesday. High\ntemperatures Tuesday will be noticeably colder than today. High\ntemperatures Tuesday will range from around 30 to the lower 30s\nnorth, to the upper 30s to around 40 Downeast. Northwest winds \nwill gust up to 25 to 30 mph Tuesday producing even colder \napparent temperatures. Apparent temperatures will range from the\nmid teens to lower 20s north, to the mid 20s to lower 30s \nDowneast.",
